  "articlePanels": [
    {"kind":"lede","html":"<p>A care home kitchen is judged twice. Environmental health inspects it as a food business and scores its hygiene. The care regulator looks at whether residents are eating well, safely and to the right texture, as part of good care. The same team, the same kitchen, two different lenses. A food hygiene audit that only satisfies one of them leaves the other exposed. The real question is how to run the audit so the evidence, temperatures, allergen controls, texture records, serves both inspectors from a single walk.</p>"},
    {"kind":"takeaways","heading":"The short version","html":"<ul><li>A care home kitchen is a food business under the Food Safety Act 1990 and food hygiene regulations, inspected by environmental health and given a Food Hygiene Rating.</li><li>Food safety rests on a HACCP-based system such as Safer Food, Better Business: temperature control, cleaning, cross-contamination and traceability.</li><li>Allergen law requires accurate information on the 14 major allergens and control of cross-contamination; residents' own allergies must be known and matched at service.</li><li>Residents with dysphagia need modified-texture food and thickened drinks produced to the correct IDDSI level, a care-specific risk a generic food audit misses.</li><li>The audit does not make the food safe; the kitchen team acting on it does.</li><li>Quickler records the audit and the evidence. It is not a clinical record and does not replace a resident's care plan or SALT recommendations.</li></ul>"},
    {"kind":"default","eyebrow":"Food safety","heading":"Temperature, hygiene and the HACCP system","html":"<p>A care home kitchen is a food business, subject to the Food Safety Act 1990 and the food hygiene regulations, and inspected by the local authority's environmental health team, who award a Food Hygiene Rating. The foundation is a documented, HACCP-based food safety management system: for many care kitchens that is the Food Standards Agency's Safer Food, Better Business, or an equivalent. It sets out the controls, cooking, chilling and hot-holding temperatures, cleaning schedules, pest control, personal hygiene, supplier and traceability, and the records that show they are being followed.</p><p>A food hygiene audit walks the kitchen against that system: are temperatures being taken and logged, is cleaning happening on schedule, is the fridge and freezer discipline sound, are the records up to date? Quickler captures each check with the probe reading or a photo where it helps, so the daily diligence is evidenced rather than assumed, and flags what has slipped. Always work from your food safety management system and the current FSA guidance, and treat this page as general information, not a substitute for it.</p>"},
    {"kind":"default","eyebrow":"Allergens","heading":"The 14 allergens, and the residents who have them","html":"<p>Allergen management in a care home has two sides. As a food business, the kitchen must hold accurate information on the 14 major allergens for the dishes it serves and control cross-contamination in storage and preparation. As a care setting, it must also know its own residents' allergies and intolerances and make sure the right resident gets the right meal, every service, including when agency staff are plating up.</p><p>The audit checks both: is the allergen information accurate and current, are controls in place to prevent cross-contamination, and is there a reliable link between a resident's known allergies and what reaches their plate? A gap here is not a paperwork issue, it can be life-threatening. Quickler records the state of each control and the evidence, so the kitchen can show both the environmental health officer and the care inspector that allergens are managed properly.</p>"},
    {"kind":"default","eyebrow":"Textures","heading":"IDDSI and safe swallowing","html":"<p>This is the check a generic food hygiene audit misses, and it is where the care regulator's interest is sharpest. Residents with dysphagia, difficulty swallowing, are at risk of choking and aspiration, and need food and drink modified to a specific texture. The International Dysphagia Diet Standardisation Initiative (IDDSI) framework defines those levels, and a resident's speech and language therapy assessment sets the level they need. The kitchen has to produce meals and thickened drinks to exactly that level, consistently.</p><p>The audit checks that the kitchen knows which resident needs which IDDSI level, that meals are produced and tested to it, and that thickened drinks are made correctly. Here the honest scope is important: quickler records the audit finding, that texture modification was checked and was being produced correctly, or was not, without holding the resident's clinical assessment. The IDDSI level and the SALT recommendation live in the resident's care plan, which stays the single source of truth. Quickler is the audit and evidence layer around it, not a clinical record.</p>"},
    {"kind":"faq","heading":"Questions, answered","html":"<h3>What does a care home kitchen audit cover?</h3><p>It covers food safety (cooking, chilling and hot-holding temperatures, cleaning, cross-contamination and traceability under a HACCP-based system), allergen management (accurate information on the 14 allergens and matching residents' allergies at service), and modified textures (producing meals and thickened drinks to the correct IDDSI level for residents with dysphagia). It serves both the environmental health inspection and the care regulator's interest in safe nutrition.</p><h3>Which regulators care about a care home kitchen?</h3><p>Two. The local authority's environmental health team inspects the kitchen as a food business under food safety law and awards a Food Hygiene Rating. Separately, the care regulator, CQC in England, the Care Inspectorate in Scotland, and the equivalents in Wales and Northern Ireland, looks at nutrition, hydration and safe swallowing as part of good care. Check the current FSA and care regulator guidance.</p><h3>Does Quickler hold a resident's IDDSI level or SALT assessment?</h3><p>No. Quickler records the audit finding, that texture modification was checked and was being produced correctly, without holding the clinical assessment. The resident's IDDSI level and speech and language therapy recommendations stay in their care plan, which remains the single source of truth. Quickler is the audit and evidence layer, not a clinical record.</p><h3>Can I run a kitchen food hygiene audit over WhatsApp?</h3><p>Yes. Quickler's workflow runs over the WhatsApp Business API. The chef or manager works through each prompt in their existing WhatsApp chat, with text, a voice note or a photo of the probe reading or allergen matrix, and the report and action list generate automatically. No separate app or login is required, and no resident clinical data is entered, because quickler is not a clinical record.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/care-home-compliance-checklist-monthly-uk.html\">Monthly care home compliance checklist</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/ipc-audit-care-home-uk.html\">IPC audit for care homes</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/care-home-audit-software-uk.html\">Care home audit software</a></li></ul>"}
